WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.768 --> 00:00:05.438
NARRATOR: Bull sharks bite with
more force, pound for pound,

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:09.075
than any other species
of large shark.

00:00:09.142 --> 00:00:11.945
But in Texas waters,
they don't frequently

00:00:12.011 --> 00:00:14.481
turn their teeth on humans.

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:18.318
That is, until 2010--

00:00:18.385 --> 00:00:21.221
three attacks in
less than a year.

00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:24.257
The safety and livelihood
of local residents

00:00:24.324 --> 00:00:26.092
is under threat.

00:00:26.159 --> 00:00:30.130
Galveston is a tourism mecca,
but having a bunch of shark

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:33.199
attacks in a short span
makes people question

00:00:33.266 --> 00:00:35.502
whether to go the beach.

00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:38.405
NARRATOR: All three attacks
take place in the waters off

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:45.211
of Galveston Island, so wildlife
journalist Chester Moore

00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:48.281
returns to the scene to
search for clues that

00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:50.850
may shed light on the mystery.

00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:55.588
And near the water's edge,
he spies something striking.

00:00:55.655 --> 00:00:57.390
CHESTER MOORE: This
is an incredible plant

00:00:57.457 --> 00:01:00.627
called Sargassum.

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:03.596
NARRATOR: Sargassum
is a floating seaweed.

00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:06.866
Much of it originates
in the Sargasso Sea,

00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:12.872
a vast 3,000-mile long stretch
of ocean in the North Atlantic.

00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:17.744
But closer to home, Chester
has noticed something sinister.

00:01:17.811 --> 00:01:23.583
This seaweed seems
to attract sharks.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.919
When I'm out there fishing
and I see a bunch of Sargassum,

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:28.254
I know I'm probably
going to catch sharks.

00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:30.924
NARRATOR: What is it
about this tiny plant that

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:34.093
appeals to sharks,
and how could it

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.163
explain the spike in attacks?

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.232
Biologist Larry
McKinney has been

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:43.536
studying the Texas coastline
for more than 40 years.

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:45.772
He says the answer
may come down to what

00:01:45.839 --> 00:01:49.108
lies hidden within Sargassum.

00:01:49.175 --> 00:01:52.812
This is a mobile habitat.

00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:54.914
NARRATOR: The seaweed
provides food,

00:01:54.981 --> 00:01:58.685
refuge, and breeding grounds
for an array of critters.

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:01.254
The types of animals that
are associated with Sargassum

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:05.859
are particularly small, starting
with small crabs and shrimp.

00:02:05.925 --> 00:02:07.794
NARRATOR: These
animals, in turn,

00:02:07.861 --> 00:02:10.930
attract a variety
of larger species

00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:17.570
like fish and sea turtles,
the preferred prey of sharks.

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:18.771
LARRY MCKINNEY: Any
place that would

00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:22.108
concentrate a food source,
sharks will always come.

00:02:22.175 --> 00:02:26.045
NARRATOR: Normally, Sargassum
floats to the Texas shore

00:02:26.112 --> 00:02:28.481
in small bits and pieces.

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:30.450
LARRY MCKINNEY: Today, we
have kind of a normal amount

00:02:30.517 --> 00:02:32.552
of Sargassum.

00:02:32.619 --> 00:02:39.692
NARRATOR: But around the time of
the attacks, something changed.

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:41.861
What I'm looking
at here are satellite

00:02:41.928 --> 00:02:48.067
photographs of the coast
of Texas during 2010, 2011.

00:02:48.134 --> 00:02:52.238
If you look just off shore, now
each one of these green dots,

00:02:52.305 --> 00:02:55.742
these are big
patches of Sargassum.

00:02:55.808 --> 00:02:57.544
They were coming in
in mats that were

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:00.213
the size of a football field.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.482
You can actually see
Sargassum from space.

00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:05.985
[music playing]

00:03:06.052 --> 00:03:07.854
NARRATOR: In
Galveston, the seaweed

00:03:07.921 --> 00:03:11.424
buries the beach in huge heaps.

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:15.128
Experts believe this
sudden influx of Sargassum

00:03:15.194 --> 00:03:19.599
may be the result of a variety
of environmental factors

00:03:19.666 --> 00:03:23.102
including strong winds
and a steady rise

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:26.239
in ocean temperatures.

00:03:26.306 --> 00:03:30.877
And the seaweed invasion
isn't limited to Texas.

00:03:30.944 --> 00:03:34.347
Beaches in Florida,
the Caribbean,

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:39.018
West Africa, and South
America are also inundated.

00:03:39.085 --> 00:03:40.386
LARRY MCKINNEY: No
matter what they tried

00:03:40.453 --> 00:03:42.188
to do to clean it up,
within a few days,

00:03:42.255 --> 00:03:44.290
more were coming ashore.

00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:47.860
NARRATOR: It's a
worldwide Sargassum siege.

00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:50.530
[music playing]

00:03:51.998 --> 00:03:55.268
Could sharks be following this
seaweed snack into the Texas

00:03:55.335 --> 00:03:59.739
shallows, thereby increasing
the risk of an accidental run-in

00:03:59.806 --> 00:04:01.674
with humans?

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:04.644
According to McKinney,
sharks do follow

00:04:04.711 --> 00:04:09.382
the mats, but only so far.

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:12.452
Well, you see, most of the
fish feeding on Sargassum

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:14.253
is out past these breakers.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.055
Once it gets up
into these sandbars,

00:04:16.122 --> 00:04:19.826
it starts rolling over and over.

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:23.262
The small animals do drop
out and they disperse.

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:25.298
NARRATOR: So once
the seaweed reaches

00:04:25.365 --> 00:04:30.870
the shallows where humans
are, it loses its appeal.

00:04:30.937 --> 00:04:35.408
So the idea that Sargassum
attracted sharks into this area

00:04:35.475 --> 00:04:36.476
is probably not very likely.

